	affiliations = {Center for Public Finance Studies (CEFIP), IIE-FCE, National University of La Plata (UNLP), La Plata, Argentina; National Academy of Economic Sciences of Argentina (ANCE), Buenos Aires, Argentina},
	abstract = {We develop a political economy framework to study how intergovernmental transfers shape subnational tax and spending decisions. Current and capital expenditures differ in their political returns and timing, while direct and indirect taxation vary in their perceived political costs and revenue technologies. The model predicts that transfers increase expenditure and, under a relative-responsiveness condition, generate a larger absolute response of current than capital expenditure. Tax rates may either increase or decrease based on the tension between the conventional resource effect and the transfer-induced rise in marginal revenue productivity, with potentially stronger responses for less salient and more responsive tax instruments (i.e., indirect taxation). We test these predictions using evidence from a highly decentralized federation (Argentina). Using a Bartik-type shift-share identification strategy, we find that transfer shocks produce a large increase in total expenditure. Both current and capital expenditure rise, with the former displaying a larger estimated response. Transfers also increase total and indirect taxation, while the response of direct taxation remains small and statistically insignificant. The qualitative findings are robust across a set of validity checks and show that transfers affect not only the size of subnational budgets but also the composition of fiscal adjustment. The evidence is consistent with the proposed framework, while remaining compatible with complementary institutional and administrative mechanisms. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
