	affiliations = {Doctor en Ciencias Económicas. Profesor asociado de la Facultad de Ciencias Económicas de la Universidad Nacional de Colombia, D.C., Bogotá, Colombia; Doctora en Contabilidad y Finanzas Corporativas, Profesora investigadora de la Facultad de Contaduría Pública de la Universidad Externado de Colombia, D.C., Bogotá, Colombia},
	abstract = {The territorial finances of Colombia are characterized by a high dependence on transfers from the national government. Some authors (Sánchez-Torres et al., 2015) interpret this as a sign of fiscal laziness; however, the empirical evidence on this issue remains inconclusive. This paper uses panel data with annual observations for approximately 1,100 municipalities between 2011 and 2017 to examine whether alternative sources of revenue substitute for municipal tax collection, or whether low tax performance is instead explained by structural factors such as limited economic and institutional capacity. The results indicate that subnational tax revenue in Colombia depends primarily on its own trajectory (j.e., fiscal inertia), on the economic capacity of territorial entities (measured by GDP per capita), and on the electoral cycle. Moreover, transfers from the General System of Participations and the General System of Royalties are not negatively associated with the municipal tax burden; rather, the relationship appears to be positive. These findings suggest that intergovernmental transfers do not necessarily discourage local tax effort, but may instead complement it, highlighting the importance of considering institutional capacity and economic context when designing policies to strengthen local fiscal autonomy. © (2026), (Universidad Pedagogica y Tecnologica de Colombia), All Rights Reserved.},
