	affiliations = {Development Pathways Ltd, United States; Skidmore College, United States},
	abstract = {In this paper, we investigate the impact of political representation in Congress in Brazil on the local provision of public goods and services. Relying on a fixed effects model, plus the fact that the electoral system generates some random variation in the political representation across terms and municipalities, we find a positive and significant effect of political representation on health and school outcomes. For instance, in health, the results indicate that one additional seat in Congress representing a municipality decreases the share of low birth weight newborns by 0.06 percentage points. When looking at school outcomes, the share of schools with access to the Internet increases by 0.63 percentage points. Moreover, the results suggest that ‘pork-barrel’ politics is one potential mechanism explaining this improvement in the provision of public goods and services. © 2021 Institute for Fiscal Studies},
